course: dictionary
lesson: 8712
title: One word, many senses
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and each meaning is called a sense. The word sense is a good example. «Rotten eggs smell» uses the first sense: your nose knows the world through smell.
explain: Sense can also mean good, sensible thinking. «Computers are fast at sums but weak at common sense, such as knowing when a joke hurts someone.» Common sense is everyday wisdom, and computers do not have it.
explain: Sense is also a verb. To sense something is to notice it without being told. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, and the words around it will guide you.
fact: A sense is one of the five ways your body knows the world: sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch.
fact: To sense something is to feel or notice it without being told.
fact: Common sense means good, sensible thinking.
fact: A sense of humour is the ability to find things funny.
fact: To make sense means to be clear and reasonable, as in «A main clause makes sense on its own.»
exercise: «Rotten eggs smell.» Which sense is the nose using? | Hearing | *Smell | Touch
exercise: «Computers are weak at common sense.» What does common sense mean here? | A kind of computer game | *Good, sensible everyday thinking | A cold, wet day | Being very loud
exercise: Mia says the word «sense» has several meanings. What is one meaning of a word called? | A question mark | *A sense of the word | A full stop | A syllable
test: In maths, «Does the answer make sense?» asks you to check something. What is it? | Whether you can hear the answer | *Whether the answer is sensible, with the right size and unit | Whether the answer is written in pencil
test: «She could sense the storm coming before the clouds came.» What does sense mean here? | Heard very loudly | Written down carefully | Forgotten completely | *Noticed without being told
exam: Which of these is NOT one of the five senses? | *Common sense | Touch | Sight | Hearing
